const content = {
    en: {
        title: "The True Path: Sacred Drums, Alpine Shadows, and the Shamans of Lugum",
        subtitle: "A 7-day Journey to East Rukum & Dhorpatan",
        heroTitle: "The Shamans of Lugum",
        heroSubtitle: "Sacred Drums, Alpine Shadows, and the Ancient Heritage of Kham Magars",
        metaDesc: "Discover the Shaman Festival (Jhakri Mela) of Lukum in East Rukum, Nepal. Sacred drums, animistic rituals, Kham Magar heritage, and alpine meadows of Dhorpatan.",
        authorName: "Buddhiman & Marga Team",
        authorRole: "Editorial Team",
        readTime: "8 min read",
        date: "August 5, 2026",
        quote: "To find the true heart of a place, you must first slow down to match its pace.",

        p1: "There are places where history is written in books. Others preserve it in monuments. But in the remote hills of eastern Rukum, history is carried in rhythm.",
        p2: "Every year, on the first Tuesday or Saturday of the Nepali month of Shrawan (Mid July), the quiet mountain village of Lugum awakens to the deep, resonant beat of the dhyangro, the traditional drum of Nepal's shamans. The villagers gather in the open ground near by the village. The fragrance of incense drifts through the cool mountain air as one after another, shamans enter the ceremonial ground wearing feathered crowns, bronze bells, traditional ornaments, colourful garments, and carrying the sacred drum that has accompanied generations before them.",
        p3: "For a visitor, the scene is mesmerizing. For the people of Lugum, it is home.",
        p4: "Far from Nepal's famous trekking trails and bustling cities, this annual gathering is not organised for tourists or performances. It is a living tradition through which the Kham Magar community honours its ancestors, strengthens family and community bonds, and preserves a spiritual heritage that has survived through generations.",
        p5: "Our seven day journey would lead us through mist-covered mountain roads, ancient villages, sacred rituals, and the silent alpine meadows of Dhorpatan. But more than a journey across landscapes, it became a journey into one of Nepal's oldest living cultures.",

        h1: "Day 1 – Kathmandu to Baglung",
        p6: "Our journey began before sunrise in Kathmandu. As the city slowly disappeared behind us, the road followed the Trishuli and Marsyangdi rivers before climbing into western Nepal. Busy highways gradually gave way to quieter mountain roads lined with terraced hillsides, cascading waterfalls, and villages perched high above deep valleys.",
        p7: "The drive to Baglung is long, yet it gently prepares travelers for what lies ahead. Every passing hour feels like another step away from modern life and closer to the slower rhythm of Nepal's mid-hills. By evening, we reached Baglung, where we rested before continuing deeper into the mountains the following morning.",

        h2: "Day 2 – Into the Homeland of the Kham Magars",
        p8: "Morning arrived wrapped in thick Himalayan fog. The mountains seemed to appear and disappear behind drifting clouds as we left Baglung and began the drive toward Lugum Village in East Rukum.",
        p9: "The first section of the road was a bit rough, with uneven terrain demanding patience. Yet every difficult stretch was rewarded by extraordinary scenery. Terraced fields climbed impossibly steep hillsides, waterfalls spilled down rocky cliffs, and scattered villages emerged quietly from the mist.",
        p10: "By late afternoon, we arrived in Lukum. Nestled among the hills, the village is home to one of Nepal's largest Kham Magar communities. Built almost entirely from locally quarried stone with traditional slate roofs, the tightly packed houses seem to grow naturally from the mountainside itself.",
        p11: "The Kham Magars are among Nepal's oldest Indigenous communities. Long before roads connected these valleys, they developed a way of life deeply rooted in the surrounding forests and mountains. Their beliefs reflect an animistic worldview in which rivers, trees, ancestors, and mountain landscapes possess living spirits deserving of respect. While many traditional practices have faded elsewhere, Lukum remains one of the few places where these customs continue to shape everyday life.",
        p12: "We stayed in a modest village guesthouse. The rooms were simple. The welcome was unforgettable. Children greeted us with cheerful smiles. The elders stopped to ask where we had travelled from. Neighbours gathered out of curiosity, eager to share stories about their village and the festival that would begin the following morning.",
        p13: "It never felt as though we were checking into accommodation. It felt as though the village itself had welcomed us home. As evening settled over the hills, excitement quietly spread through Lukum. Tomorrow will be the most important day of the year.",

        h3: "Day 3 – The Great Shaman Festival",
        p14: "Before dawn, villagers already started their preparation for the festival. The steady beat of the dhyangro echoed through the valley while curls of incense drifted into the cool mountain air. Families arrived together. Relatives came from neighbouring villages. Children watched with anticipation as elders prepared for the day's ceremonies.",
        p15: "One by one, the shamans started to gather to prepare for the big day. Each wore magnificent feathered headdresses crafted from Himalayan monal and pheasant feathers, bronze bells around their necks, colourful ceremonial garments, sacred ornaments, and carried the dhyangro, drum that would accompany them throughout the day.",
        p16: "The annual Shaman Festival is held on the first Tuesday or Saturday of Shrawan ( mid July), bringing together not only the people of Lukum but also relatives, people from neighbouring villages, and few international visitors. For the Kham Magar community, it is one of the year's most important cultural gatherings, a time to honour ancestors, strengthen social ties, pass on sacred knowledge and protection from the evil spirits, and celebrate an identity that has endured through generations.",
        p17: "While shamanic traditions have gradually disappeared from many parts of Nepal, Lukum continues to preserve them as a vibrant part of everyday life. Today, dozens of shamans remain active within the village, and one remarkable aspect of the tradition is that women serve as shamans alongside men, reflecting a long-standing practice in which spiritual leadership is shared across genders.",
        p18: "Sacred chants, rituals, and ceremonial knowledge are passed from teacher to disciple, often within the same family, ensuring that each generation inherits not only techniques but also the responsibility of protecting the community's cultural heritage.",

        subH1: "The Legend Behind the Festival",
        p19: "During our stay, we had the privilege to have a conversation with Bir Prasad Gharti Magar, a local resident who is promoting the Lugum village and its intangible culture through his content creation. He generously shared the stories and traditions that have been passed down through generations. Like many Indigenous cultures, the Kham Magars preserve much of their history through oral storytelling, and one legend in particular continues to shape the meaning of the festival.",
        p20: "According to Bir Prasad, a long time ago a powerful evil spirit terrorised the village, bringing fear and death to its people. Unable to live in peace, the villagers sought help from a great shaman from the village. A fierce spiritual battle followed, lasting until the shaman finally imprisoned the evil spirit in the earth at the edge of the village.",
        p21: "Enraged by her defeat, the evil spirit cursed both the shaman and the people of Lukum. The shaman, however, negotiated with the evil spirit, and an agreement was reached : once every year, the spirit would be temporarily released before being bound once again, this is the day celebrated as the Shaman Festival (Jhakri Mela). Even today, villagers believe that if someone passes away shortly after the festival, it is because the evil spirit claimed the life promised under that ancient agreement.",
        p22: "Whether understood as folklore, spiritual belief, or cultural memory, the legend remains an important part of the village's identity and continues to be told from one generation to the next.",

        subH2: "A Village of Shamans",
        p23: "One of Lukum's most unique traditions is the close relationship between every household and the community of shamans. Historically, each family is expected to have a shaman to represent them during the festival. If a household does not have one within the family. Then, they either hire another shaman to represent them or contribute a traditional fine to the shaman community. In this way, every household participates in maintaining the village's spiritual and cultural responsibilities.",
        p24: "Throughout the day, more than dozens of  shamans performed sacred rituals and dances almost without interruption.",
        p25: "The rhythmic beat of the drums gradually intensified. Bronze bells rang with every movement. Feet struck the earth in perfect rhythm. Ancient chants echoed across the village square. Hour after hour, the shamans danced in remarkable unison, their movements expressing centuries of inherited tradition rather than choreographed performance.",
        p26: "Standing among the crowd, it became clear that this gathering was far more than a festival. It was a living archive. Every feather. Every drumbeat. Every chant. Every dance. Together they carried the memory of generations who had gathered on the very same ground long before us.",
        p27: "As the final rhythms faded into the evening mist, the ceremonies for the day came to an end. But the festival itself was only halfway complete. The following morning, the atmosphere would change entirely. The solemn rituals would give way to music, laughter, playful traditions, and celebrations that would fill the entire village.",

        h4: "Day 4 – Celebration, Purification, and the Blessings",
        p28: "If the previous day belonged to the spirits, The fourth day belonged to the people. The atmosphere in Lukum changed completely. The solemn chants gave way to laughter. The slow rhythm of ceremonial drums became lively folk beats, and the entire village gathered once again not only to celebrate the successful completion of the sacred rituals but also to enjoy one another's company.",
        p29: "The shamans' assistants, many of them younger apprentices learning the traditions from their elders, took centre stage. Their energetic drumming echoed across the village square as they playfully teased the young women through songs, dance, and light-hearted exchanges that have long been part of the festival's social tradition.",
        p30: "Children chased one another through the narrow lanes. Elders laughed from shaded verandas. Visitors who had arrived only as spectators soon found themselves smiling alongside everyone else.",

        subH3: "The Sisno Tradition",
        p31: "One of the festival's most distinctive customs is the Sisno (stinging nettle) ritual. Fresh nettles are gently used by the villagers as they playfully strike one another. To an outsider, the practice may seem unusual, but for the people of Lukum it carries deep symbolic meaning.",
        p32: "The community believes the ritual helps cleanse the body, remove negative influences, and offer protection from illness and misfortune in the coming year. What appears playful on the surface is, for the villagers, another way of renewing both body and spirit.",

        subH4: "A Festival of Mud, Laughter, and Equality",
        p33: "Soon afterwards, another celebration unfolded. Without warning, handfuls of cool mountain earth began flying through the air. Children covered their parents. Friends chased one another through the village square. Even visitors were not spared if they were ready to be part of the celebration. Within minutes, everyone was covered in mud.",
        p34: "The celebration immediately reminded us of Holi, but instead of coloured powder, the people of Lukum celebrate with the very soil beneath their feet.",
        p35: "The earth is more than mud. It represents the land that feeds the community, the fields that sustain every family, and the reminder that regardless of age, wealth, or social standing, everyone ultimately returns to the same ground.",
        p36: "Covered in mud, distinctions disappear. Everyone becomes equal. The square filled with nothing but laughter. For a few beautiful hours, the village celebrated life itself.",

        subH5: "Protection and Blessing Every Home",
        p37: "As the public celebrations gradually came to an end, another important tradition quietly began. Unlike the ceremonies held in the village square, this ritual takes place behind the doors of every household. One by one, the shamans travelled from house to house across the village.",
        p38: "According to local tradition, every family receives the blessings and protection of the shamans before the festival concludes. The purpose is to cleanse the negative energy, protect the household from evil spirits, and pray for the good health, prosperity, and well-being of every family member during the year ahead.",
        p39: "By this stage, many of the shamans had entered deep states of spiritual trance. Their assistants carefully guided them from one house to the next, carrying ritual objects and helping prepare each ceremony. In every home, sacred chants filled the rooms while offerings were made.",
        p40: "As part of the traditional ritual, a chicken was ceremonially sacrificed in each household. For the community, the offering represents gratitude, protection, and the renewal of the bond between the family, their ancestors, and the unseen spiritual world.",
        p41: "It is an intensely personal ceremony. Visitors are welcomed with respect, but the rituals remain deeply rooted in the lives of the families themselves.",
        p42: "Walking through the village that evening, it became clear that the festival is not something performed in one public square. It belongs to every household. Every family. Every generation.",
        p43: "By nightfall, the sound of the drums slowly faded. The village became quiet once more. The annual gathering had come to an end, but its blessings and protection would remain with the community until the following Shrawan.",

        h5: "Day 5 – The Road to Burtibang",
        p44: "The following morning, we said goodbye to Lukum. The roads once again wound through forests, river valleys, and mountains as we travelled toward Burtibang, the gateway to one of Nepal's most remarkable alpine landscapes.",
        p45: "Leaving the village felt strangely emotional. Only a day earlier, the streets had been filled with music, chanting, and celebration. Now they were peaceful once again, returning to the quiet rhythm of everyday life.",
        p46: "As the kilometres passed, the scenery gradually changed. Steep hills gave way to wider valleys. Dense forests opened into broad mountain landscapes. Every turn revealed another dramatic view stretching toward the distant Himalayas.",
        p47: "Burtibang has long served as an important stop for travellers, traders, and shepherds moving through western Nepal. Today, it is best known as the starting point for journeys into the spectacular highlands of Dhorpatan.",
        p48: "After checking into our accommodation, we rested for the night, preparing for an early departure the following morning.",

        h6: "Day 6 – The Silent Meadows of Dhorpatan",
        p49: "Before sunrise, we were already on the road. A two-hour drive carried us steadily upward until the landscape suddenly opened into something entirely different. The mountains seemed to step back. The valleys widened. The horizon stretched endlessly. We had arrived in Dhorpatan.",
        p50: "For generations, these vast alpine grasslands have served as seasonal grazing grounds for shepherds who bring their flocks into the high Himalayas during the warmer months. Even today, sheep continue to roam freely across the meadows, watched over by shepherds whose lives remain closely connected to the rhythm of the mountains.",
        p51: "Unlike the dramatic peaks that often define Nepal in photographs, Dhorpatan offers something quieter. Rolling grasslands. Crystal-clear streams. Wetlands reflecting the sky. Snow-covered mountains standing silently in the distance.",
        p52: "We spent the morning wandering through Patan, the expansive alpine meadows that give Dhorpatan its distinctive character.",
        p53: "The contrast with Lukum could not have been greater. Just two days earlier, our ears had been filled with the relentless rhythm of drums, chanting, bells, and celebration. Here... There was only wind. The occasional sound of sheep bells. The whisper of grass moving beneath the breeze.",
        p54: "The silence felt almost sacred. It invited reflection. The festival had reminded us of the strength of community. Dhorpatan reminded us of the quiet power of nature.",
        p55: "After several peaceful hours exploring the high meadows, we began the drive back toward Kusma. The mountains slowly disappeared behind us, but the stillness they offered remained long after we had left.",

        h7: "More Than a Festival",
        p56: "Many visitors arrive in Nepal hoping to see towering mountains. Others come searching for famous trekking trails. Few realise that some of the country's greatest treasures are not landscapes at all. They are its people.",
        p57: "The Shaman Festival (Jhakri Mela) of Lukum is remarkable not simply because more than dozens of shamans perform spiritual rituals and dance together from early morning until evening. Not because of feathered crowns, rhythmic drums, or ancient rituals. Its true significance lies elsewhere.",
        p58: "It is one of the rare places where an Indigenous tradition continues to thrive not as a performance, but as everyday life. Children grow up watching their grandparents participate. Women serve alongside men as respected spiritual leaders. Entire families gather each year to honour their ancestors, renew community bonds, protect the village and pass their traditions to the next generation.",
        p59: "In a rapidly changing world, that continuity is extraordinary. The festival is not a recreation of the past. It is the present. A living culture that continues to evolve while remaining deeply connected to its roots.",

        h8: "The True Path",
        p60: "When we first set out from Kathmandu, we thought we were travelling to witness a festival. Instead, we found something far more meaningful. We found a community that welcomed strangers as guests. We found traditions that have endured despite the passing of centuries. We found mountain landscapes where silence speaks as powerfully as music. And we found that the richest journeys are often the ones that ask us not to conquer a place, but to listen to it.",
        p61: "In Nepali, \"Marga\" means the path. Not simply a road from one destination to another, but a way of moving through the world with curiosity, humility, and respect.",
        p62: "Looking back, that is what this journey became. The road to Lukum was a bit rough somewhere. The drums were loud. The mountains were vast. The stories were ancient. And somewhere between the mist-covered hills of Rukum and the quiet alpine meadows of Dhorpatan, we realised that the true destination had never been a village or a festival. It was a deeper understanding of the people who call these mountains home.",
        p63: "As the final echoes of the dhyangro faded into memory and the highway carried us back to Kathmandu, one feeling remained. Some journeys end when you return home. The meaningful ones continue long after the road has ended.",

        prev: "Previous Story",
        back: "Back to Journal",
        next: "Next Story",

        caption0: "Collage – The Shamans of Lugum",
        caption1: "Scenic highway leaving Kathmandu with rivers, terraced hills and mountain landscapes",
        caption2: "Fog-covered mountain road between Baglung and Rukum",
        caption3: "Traditional slate-roofed stone houses of Lukum Village",
        caption4: "Villagers gathering as shamans enter the ceremonial ground",
        caption5: "A group of shamans performing together with dhyangro drums",
        caption6: "A young boy portraying the shaman while villagers gather around",
        caption7: "Villagers participating in the Sisno ritual",
        caption8: "Villagers joyfully throwing mud and laughing together",
        caption9: "Protecting home",
        caption10: "A beautiful waterfall between Lukum and Burtibang",
